A `for` loop in C++ has three parts in its header — an initializer, a condition, and an increment — and executes its body repeatedly until the condition is false [1](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-s096-introduction-to-c-and-c-january-iap-2013/pages/lectures-and-assignments/):

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    // body executes while i < 10
}
```

